-- title:
--   Double-cone membership is invariant under positive scaling
-- statement:
--   Work in $d$-dimensional Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$, and for a unit vector $v$ and an angle
--   $\vartheta$ let $V(v,\vartheta)$ denote the double cone `doubleCone v ϑ`, the set of non-zero points
--   whose direction makes an angle **strictly less than** $\vartheta$ with $v$ or with $-v$:
--   $$V(v,\vartheta) \;=\; \{\,h \ne 0 : \langle v,h\rangle/\|h\| > \cos\vartheta\,\} \cup \bigl(-\{\,h \ne 0 : \langle v,h\rangle/\|h\| > \cos\vartheta\,\}\bigr).$$
--
--   Let $h > 0$ be a real scalar, let $v \in \mathbb{R}^d$ with $\|v\| = 1$, and let $\vartheta$
--   satisfy $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$. Then for **every** point $p \in \mathbb{R}^d$,
--
--   $$h\,p \in V(v,\vartheta) \iff p \in V(v,\vartheta).$$
--
--   This is the two-sided form of the dilation invariance of double cones, stated for an arbitrary
--   point $p$ (the degenerate point $p = 0$ included, where both sides are false since the origin lies in no double cone). It is the form needed when a cone condition is
--   to be checked after passing from the scaled lattice $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ back to $\mathbb{Z}^d$, where
--   the implication is used in the direction that strips the scaling factor.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The scalar is written $h$ because it plays the role of the mesh of the
--   scaled lattice; the statement quantifies over $p$ explicitly rather than taking it implicitly, so
--   the equivalence can be rewritten with directly.
